# 9. Delivery Methodology

Klaud follows a structured delivery methodology designed to reduce risk, improve execution quality, and provide partners with a repeatable model for complex VMware Cloud Foundation and private cloud projects.

The methodology is practical and outcome-driven. It is designed to support professional services delivery across assessments, architecture, deployments, upgrades, migrations, add-ons, and stabilization engagements.

While each project may vary depending on scope and complexity, Klaud generally follows the phases below.

---

## 9.1 Discover

The discovery phase is used to understand the customer’s current environment, business drivers, technical requirements, constraints, and project objectives.

Typical activities may include:

* Technical discovery sessions
* Review of current infrastructure
* Review of VMware, network, storage, and security architecture
* Identification of business and technical drivers
* Review of project goals and success criteria
* Identification of key stakeholders
* Initial risk and dependency review
* Collection of required technical information

The objective of this phase is to establish a clear understanding of the current state and the expected outcome.

---

## 9.2 Assess

The assessment phase validates whether the environment is ready for the proposed service or project.

Typical activities may include:

* Version and compatibility review
* Hardware readiness review
* Licensing assumption review
* Network prerequisite review
* DNS, NTP, certificate, and identity prerequisite review
* vSphere, vSAN, NSX, or VCF health review
* Upgrade or migration readiness analysis
* Identification of technical gaps
* Risk classification and prioritization

The objective of this phase is to identify issues early, reduce delivery risk, and confirm whether remediation is required before implementation.

---

## 9.3 Design

The design phase defines the target architecture and the technical approach required to deliver the expected outcome.

Typical activities may include:

* High-level architecture definition
* Low-level technical design
* Workload domain planning
* vSphere, vSAN, and NSX design
* Security and segmentation design
* Lifecycle management design
* Automation and operations design
* Disaster recovery or migration design
* Validation of assumptions and constraints
* Design review with partner and customer stakeholders

The objective of this phase is to create a clear and approved technical blueprint before implementation begins.

---

## 9.4 Plan

The planning phase translates the approved design into an executable delivery plan.

Typical activities may include:

* Implementation planning
* Deployment sequencing
* Change window planning
* Migration or upgrade planning
* Rollback planning
* Resource alignment
* Risk and issue review
* Prerequisite tracking
* Validation checklist preparation
* Partner and customer responsibility alignment

The objective of this phase is to ensure that all required people, access, systems, prerequisites, and approvals are ready before execution.

---

## 9.5 Deploy and Configure

The deployment phase includes the implementation, configuration, upgrade, migration, or remediation activities defined in the agreed scope.

Typical activities may include:

* VMware Cloud Foundation deployment
* Workload domain implementation
* vSphere configuration
* vSAN configuration
* NSX configuration
* VCF Operations integration
* VCF Automation setup
* VKS enablement
* VMware Live Recovery / SRM implementation
* HCX enablement
* NSX ALB / AVI deployment
* vDefend Firewall starter implementation
* Upgrade execution
* Migration support
* Stabilization or remediation activities

The objective of this phase is to deliver the agreed technical scope according to the approved design and implementation plan.

---

## 9.6 Validate

The validation phase confirms that the delivered components are operating as expected and that the agreed success criteria have been met.

Typical activities may include:

* Post-deployment validation
* Health checks
* Connectivity validation
* Cluster and workload domain validation
* vSAN health validation
* NSX routing and connectivity validation
* Lifecycle management validation
* Automation or operations validation
* DR or migration validation, when applicable
* Review of known issues or remaining risks

The objective of this phase is to confirm technical completion and identify any remaining items before handover.

---

## 9.7 Document

The documentation phase provides a record of the delivered solution, configuration, findings, and recommendations.

Depending on the engagement scope, documentation may include:

* Technical findings report
* Readiness report
* High-level design
* Low-level design
* Implementation runbook
* Validation checklist
* As-built documentation
* Risk and issue summary
* Operational recommendations
* Handover notes

The objective of this phase is to support operational continuity and give the partner and customer a clear reference for the delivered work.

---

## 9.8 Transfer Knowledge

The knowledge transfer phase helps the partner and/or customer understand the delivered solution and how to operate it after project completion.

Typical activities may include:

* Technical handover session
* Review of architecture and design decisions
* Review of implemented components
* Review of operational considerations
* Review of validation results
* Review of documentation
* Q&A session with technical teams
* Recommendations for next steps

The objective of this phase is to reduce dependency on the implementation team and improve operational confidence.

---

## 9.9 Hypercare and Stabilization

Some fixed-price bundles may include a limited hypercare period after delivery. Hypercare is intended to support stabilization and clarify items related to the agreed scope.

Typical activities may include:

* Follow-up technical questions
* Issue triage related to delivered scope
* Minor configuration review
* Clarification of documentation
* Validation support
* Handover support

Hypercare is not a replacement for managed services, 24x7 support, or long-term operations. Any additional work outside the agreed scope may be handled through hourly services, Expert Bank hours, or a separate engagement.

---

## 9.10 Delivery Governance

Klaud uses lightweight delivery governance to keep the engagement aligned, transparent, and controlled.

Depending on the engagement, this may include:

* Kickoff meeting
* Defined technical contacts
* Scope confirmation
* Milestone tracking
* Risk and issue tracking
* Regular status updates
* Change control
* Deliverable review
* Closure confirmation

The partner remains responsible for the overall customer relationship, commercial governance, and customer-facing project ownership unless otherwise agreed.

---

## 9.11 Methodology Summary

Klaud’s delivery methodology can be summarized as:

| Phase                  | Purpose                                     | Typical Output                               |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| **Discover**           | Understand the current state and objectives | Discovery notes, requirements, initial risks |
| **Assess**             | Validate readiness and identify gaps        | Readiness findings, risk register            |
| **Design**             | Define the target architecture              | HLD, LLD, design decisions                   |
| **Plan**               | Prepare for execution                       | Implementation plan, runbook, rollback plan  |
| **Deploy / Configure** | Deliver the agreed technical scope          | Implemented solution                         |
| **Validate**           | Confirm successful delivery                 | Validation checklist, health checks          |
| **Document**           | Record what was delivered                   | As-built documentation, findings report      |
| **Transfer Knowledge** | Enable partner/customer teams               | Handover session, Q&A                        |
| **Hypercare**          | Support early stabilization                 | Follow-up support within agreed scope        |

This methodology allows Klaud to deliver complex VMware and private cloud services in a structured, repeatable, and partner-friendly way while maintaining flexibility for different project types and customer environments.
